# apps/control-panel/Dockerfile.dockerignore -- the build context of the control panel image, and ONLY of it.
#
# BuildKit reads `<Dockerfile name>.dockerignore` next to the Dockerfile in place of the root .dockerignore, for
# that one Dockerfile. This image copies the whole repository into its builder stage (`COPY . /codemap-src`) so
# that the code map can be made from the code, and a copy of everything must not carry a secret. So this file is
# an ALLOW-LIST: the first rule excludes everything, the next lines let the source folders back in, and the
# last lines exclude again what must never be in a build context. Those last lines mirror what the root .gitignore marks
# as secret, plus the per-run test state that nested .gitignore files name: a `secrets` folder at any depth, every `.env*` and `*.env` file, key, certificate and keystore files,
# ssh keys, kubeconfig files, backups, the generated or host-owned files (livekit.prod.yaml, the pgbouncer user list,
# the keycloak realm export, the element-call config, the compose pins and local compose files) and the Playwright
# sweep scratch. When .gitignore gains a new secret path, add it here too (dockerignore.test.mjs derives the secret
# classes from .gitignore and fails when one is not covered). A new top-level folder stays out until it is added here.
#
# Those rules hide more tracked files than the map needs, so the image map has a few fewer L0 file nodes than the CI map.
# Two of the hidden files are the only files of their areas: compose/ci.placeholder.env (a placeholder, no value) and
# docker/cloudflared/origin-ca-root.pem (a public root certificate). They are the only files of their areas in the
# code map, so the last two lines of this file let them back in. dockerignore.test.mjs checks that the rules hide
# no area of the code map; it does not check that every tracked file gets in.
#
# BuildKit is the supported builder for this image. Under DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0 (the legacy builder) this file is
# ignored and the root .dockerignore applies. That one filters far fewer secret classes (no key, certificate,
# keystore, ssh key, kubeconfig or backups rules, for example), so such a file can land in a discarded builder layer
# (the COPY layer of the builder stage), never in the runner stage, which copies only the build output and the
# status file. The code map then also reads its commit as "unknown" and has fewer L0 files, and nothing fails
# (apps/control-panel/codemap/README.md).
*

!apps
!compose
!docker
!docker-compose*.yml
!gremion-public
!gremion-ui
!infra
!k8s
!modules
!ops
!packages
!services
!scripts
!test
!LICENSE
!NOTICE
!package.json
!pnpm-lock.yaml
!pnpm-workspace.yaml

# the commit, without a git program: HEAD, and the refs that HEAD can point at
!.git/HEAD
!.git/packed-refs
!.git/refs

**/node_modules
**/.svelte-kit
**/build
**/coverage
gremion-ui/_module-lane
**/.env*
**/*.env
**/*.env.local
**/*.key
**/*.pem
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/secrets
docker/livekit/livekit.prod.yaml

# the rest of what .gitignore marks as secret or as host-owned output, at any depth
**/*.crt
**/*.csr
**/*.srl
**/*.jks
**/*.keystore
**/id_rsa*
**/id_ed25519*
**/*.kubeconfig
**/.kube
**/backups
**/.claude
**/CLAUDE.md
**/TEST_USERS.md
**/_design_api
**/docker-compose.g080-throwaway.yml
**/g080-throwaway-realm.json
**/docker-compose.local-testdb.yml
docker-compose.pins.yml
docker-compose.state-pins.yml
docker/pgbouncer/userlist.txt
docker/keycloak/realm-export.json
docker/element-call/config.json
gremion-ui/finance/migration/reports
gremion-ui/sweep-*

# Per-run test state that nested .gitignore files name (throwaway keys, connection state): absent from a clean clone.
**/.proof-root
**/.t046-tmp
**/.testdb-state.json
**/.stack-state.json
**/docker-compose.local-testdb.*.yml
# LAST, so that nothing above hides them again: the tracked public files that the rules above hide (see the header)
!compose/ci.placeholder.env
!docker/cloudflared/origin-ca-root.pem
